@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Collections in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">

    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <div class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Collections in Java</h2>

            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn the Java Collections Framework, including List, Set,
                Map, Queue, Deque, ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet,
                HashMap, iteration, sorting, and common collection operations.
            </p>
        </div>


        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">What is the Collections Framework?</h3>

            <p>
                The Java Collections Framework provides interfaces and
                implementations for storing and manipulating groups of
                objects.
            </p>

            <p>
                The main collection interfaces include
                <code>List</code>, <code>Set</code>, <code>Queue</code>,
                <code>Deque</code>, and <code>Map</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");

System.out.println(names);</code></pre>

            </div>
            List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");

System.out.println(names);

        </section>


        <!-- COLLECTION HIERARCHY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Collection Framework Overview</h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Structure</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Collection
│
├── List
│   ├── ArrayList
│   └── LinkedList
│
├── Set
│   ├── HashSet
│   ├── LinkedHashSet
│   └── TreeSet
│
└── Queue
    ├── PriorityQueue
    └── Deque
        └── ArrayDeque

Map
├── HashMap
├── LinkedHashMap
└── TreeMap</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

                <strong>Important:</strong>

                <code>Map</code> is part of the Java Collections Framework,
                but it does not extend the <code>Collection</code> interface.

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- LIST -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">List</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>List</code> is an ordered collection that permits
                duplicate elements and provides index-based access.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");
names.add("Amit");

System.out.println(names);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>[Amit, Neha, Amit]</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- ARRAYLIST -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">ArrayList</h3>

            <p>
                <code>ArrayList</code> is a resizable-array implementation of
                the <code>List</code> interface.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; languages =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

languages.add("Java");
languages.add("Python");
languages.add("C#");

System.out.println(
    languages.get(0)
);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                ArrayList is generally a good default when you need indexed
                access and frequent access by position.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- ARRAYLIST OPERATIONS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Common ArrayList Operations
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");

names.add(1, "Rahul");

names.set(0, "Priya");

names.remove("Neha");

System.out.println(names);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>[Priya, Rahul]</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- LINKEDLIST -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">LinkedList</h3>

            <p>
                <code>LinkedList</code> implements both
                <code>List</code> and <code>Deque</code>. It can be useful
                when operations at the ends are important.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>LinkedList&lt;String&gt; names =
    new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.addFirst("Neha");
names.addLast("Rahul");

System.out.println(names);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>[Neha, Amit, Rahul]</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- SET -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Set</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>Set</code> does not permit duplicate elements.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Set&lt;String&gt; languages =
    new HashSet&lt;&gt;();

languages.add("Java");
languages.add("Python");
languages.add("Java");

System.out.println(languages);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The duplicate <code>"Java"</code> value is not added again.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- HASHSET -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">HashSet</h3>

            <p>
                <code>HashSet</code> is a hash-table-based implementation of
                <code>Set</code>. It does not guarantee iteration order.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Set&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new HashSet&lt;&gt;();

numbers.add(30);
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(10);

System.out.println(numbers);</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- LINKEDHASHSET -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">LinkedHashSet</h3>

            <p>
                <code>LinkedHashSet</code> maintains insertion order during
                iteration.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Set&lt;String&gt; names =
    new LinkedHashSet&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");
names.add("Rahul");

System.out.println(names);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>[Amit, Neha, Rahul]</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- TREESET -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">TreeSet</h3>

            <p>
                <code>TreeSet</code> is a sorted set implementation based on
                a tree structure.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Set&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new TreeSet&lt;&gt;();

numbers.add(50);
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(30);
numbers.add(20);

System.out.println(numbers);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>[10, 20, 30, 50]</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- QUEUE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Queue</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>Queue</code> is commonly used when elements need to
                be processed according to a particular ordering, often
                FIFO.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Queue&lt;String&gt; queue =
    new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();

queue.offer("A");
queue.offer("B");
queue.offer("C");

System.out.println(queue.poll());</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>A</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PRIORITY QUEUE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">PriorityQueue</h3>

            <p>
                <code>PriorityQueue</code> processes elements according to
                their priority rather than simple insertion order.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Queue&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();

numbers.offer(30);
numbers.offer(10);
numbers.offer(20);

System.out.println(numbers.poll());</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>10</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- DEQUE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Deque</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>Deque</code> supports insertion and removal at both
                ends of the sequence.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Deque&lt;String&gt; deque =
    new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();

deque.addFirst("A");
deque.addLast("B");

System.out.println(
    deque.removeFirst()
);

System.out.println(
    deque.removeLast()
);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>A
B</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- MAP -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Map</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>Map</code> stores key-value associations. Keys are
                unique within a map.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; students =
    new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

students.put(101, "Amit");
students.put(102, "Neha");

System.out.println(
    students.get(101)
);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- HASHMAP -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">HashMap</h3>

            <p>
                <code>HashMap</code> is a commonly used map implementation
                based on hashing. It does not guarantee iteration order.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; scores =
    new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

scores.put("Amit", 90);
scores.put("Neha", 85);

scores.put("Amit", 95);

System.out.println(
    scores.get("Amit")
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>95</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Adding a value with an existing key replaces the previous
                value associated with that key.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- LINKEDHASHMAP -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">LinkedHashMap</h3>

            <p>
                <code>LinkedHashMap</code> maintains a predictable iteration
                order, normally based on insertion order unless configured
                for access order.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; scores =
    new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();

scores.put("Amit", 90);
scores.put("Neha", 85);
scores.put("Rahul", 88);

System.out.println(scores);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>{Amit=90, Neha=85, Rahul=88}</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- TREEMAP -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">TreeMap</h3>

            <p>
                <code>TreeMap</code> maintains its keys in sorted order
                according to their natural ordering or a supplied comparator.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; students =
    new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();

students.put(103, "Rahul");
students.put(101, "Amit");
students.put(102, "Neha");

System.out.println(students);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>{101=Amit, 102=Neha, 103=Rahul}</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- ITERATION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Iterating Through a Collection
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of("Amit", "Neha", "Rahul");

for (String name : names) {

    System.out.println(name);

}</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit
Neha
Rahul</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- ITERATOR -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Iterator</h3>

            <p>
                An <code>Iterator</code> provides a standard way to traverse
                many collection types.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");
names.add("Rahul");

Iterator&lt;String&gt; iterator =
    names.iterator();

while (iterator.hasNext()) {

    String name = iterator.next();

    System.out.println(name);

}</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit
Neha
Rahul</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- REMOVE WITH ITERATOR -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Removing Elements with Iterator
            </h3>

            <p>
                An iterator can safely remove elements from the collection
                during iteration using its <code>remove()</code> method.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(30);

Iterator&lt;Integer&gt; iterator =
    numbers.iterator();

while (iterator.hasNext()) {

    Integer number = iterator.next();

    if (number == 20) {

        iterator.remove();

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SORTING -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Sorting Collections</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>Collections.sort()</code> method can sort a mutable
                list according to natural ordering.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(
        List.of(50, 10, 30, 20)
    );

Collections.sort(numbers);

System.out.println(numbers);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>[10, 20, 30, 50]</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- COMPARATOR -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Sorting with Comparator</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>Comparator</code> allows custom ordering.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(
        List.of(10, 20, 30, 40)
    );

numbers.sort(
    Comparator.reverseOrder()
);

System.out.println(numbers);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>[40, 30, 20, 10]</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- MAP ITERATION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Iterating Through a Map
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; scores =
    Map.of(
        "Amit", 90,
        "Neha", 85
    );

for (
    Map.Entry&lt;String, Integer&gt; entry
    : scores.entrySet()
) {

    System.out.println(
        entry.getKey()
        + " = "
        + entry.getValue()
    );

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- MAP METHODS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Useful Map Methods
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; scores =
    new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

scores.put("Amit", 90);

scores.containsKey("Amit");

scores.containsValue(90);

scores.get("Amit");

scores.getOrDefault(
    "Neha",
    0
);

scores.remove("Amit");</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- GET OR DEFAULT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                getOrDefault()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>getOrDefault()</code> returns a fallback value when a
                requested key is not present.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; scores =
    new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

scores.put("Amit", 90);

int score = scores.getOrDefault(
    "Neha",
    0
);

System.out.println(score);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>0</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- MAP COMPUTE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                computeIfAbsent()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>computeIfAbsent()</code> can create and store a value
                when a key does not already have a mapping.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;String, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; groups =
    new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

groups.computeIfAbsent(
    "Java",
    key -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()
).add("Amit");

System.out.println(groups);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>{Java=[Amit]}</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- IMMUTABLE COLLECTIONS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Immutable Collection Factories
            </h3>

            <p>
                Modern Java provides convenient factory methods such as
                <code>List.of()</code>, <code>Set.of()</code>, and
                <code>Map.of()</code> for creating unmodifiable collections.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of("Amit", "Neha");

Set&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    Set.of(10, 20, 30);

Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; students =
    Map.of(
        101, "Amit",
        102, "Neha"
    );</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

                These factory-created collections do not support structural
                modification such as <code>add()</code> or
                <code>remove()</code>.

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- NULL -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Null Values
            </h3>

            <p>
                Collection implementations differ in how they handle
                <code>null</code>. For example, HashMap permits a null key
                and null values, while TreeMap's behavior depends on the
                ordering configuration.
            </p>

            <p>
                Always check the contract of the particular collection
                implementation when null handling matters.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- CHOOSING COLLECTION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Choosing the Right Collection
            </h3>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Requirement</th>
                            <th>Common Choice</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Indexed ordered data</td>
                            <td>ArrayList</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Unique values</td>
                            <td>HashSet</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Unique sorted values</td>
                            <td>TreeSet</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Insertion-order set</td>
                            <td>LinkedHashSet</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Key-value lookup</td>
                            <td>HashMap</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Sorted keys</td>
                            <td>TreeMap</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>FIFO-style processing</td>
                            <td>Queue</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Both-end operations</td>
                            <td>Deque / ArrayDeque</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- COLLECTION VS ARRAY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Collections vs Arrays
            </h3>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Feature</th>
                            <th>Array</th>
                            <th>Collection</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Size</td>
                            <td>Fixed</td>
                            <td>Usually dynamic</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Primitive Elements</td>
                            <td>Supported</td>
                            <td>Use wrapper types</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Built-in Operations</td>
                            <td>Limited</td>
                            <td>Rich API</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Generics</td>
                            <td>Array component types</td>
                            <td>Strongly integrated</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- REAL WORLD -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Employee Management
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; employees =
    new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

employees.put(101, "Amit");
employees.put(102, "Neha");
employees.put(103, "Rahul");

for (
    Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt; entry
    : employees.entrySet()
) {

    System.out.println(
        entry.getKey()
        + " : "
        + entry.getValue()
    );

}</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>101 : Amit
102 : Neha
103 : Rahul</code></pre>
</div>

     <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
          <h5 class="fw-bold">Important:</h5>
         <p> The iteration order of `HashMap` is not guaranted, so the exact order is not fixed.</p>

          </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PERFORMANCE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Performance Considerations
            </h3>

            <p>
                Different collection implementations provide different
                performance characteristics.
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <code>ArrayList</code> provides efficient indexed access.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <code>HashMap</code> is commonly used for fast key-based
                    lookup under typical conditions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <code>TreeMap</code> and <code>TreeSet</code> maintain
                    sorted order with logarithmic-style tree operations.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Choose based on required operations rather than assuming
                    one collection is always fastest.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Collections Best Practices
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Declare variables using interfaces such as
                    <code>List</code>, <code>Set</code>, and <code>Map</code>
                    where practical.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Choose an implementation based on ordering, uniqueness,
                    lookup, and update requirements.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use generics to maintain compile-time type safety.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid raw collection types.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Prefer unmodifiable collections when mutation is not
                    required.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- INTERVIEW -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Interview Questions
            </h3>

            <div class="accordion" id="collectionQuestions">

                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#collectionQuestionOne">

                            What is the Java Collections Framework?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="collectionQuestionOne"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#collectionQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            It is a set of interfaces, implementations, and
                            utility algorithms for working with groups of
                            objects.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#collectionQuestionTwo">

                            What is the difference between List and Set?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="collectionQuestionTwo"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#collectionQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            A List is ordered and permits duplicates, while a
                            Set does not permit duplicate elements.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#collectionQuestionThree">

                            What is the difference between ArrayList and LinkedList?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="collectionQuestionThree"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#collectionQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            ArrayList is backed by a resizable array and is
                            generally strong for indexed access. LinkedList
                            is a doubly linked list and also implements
                            Deque, making operations at the ends convenient.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#collectionQuestionFour">

                            HashMap vs TreeMap?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="collectionQuestionFour"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#collectionQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            HashMap is hash-based and does not guarantee key
                            iteration order. TreeMap maintains keys in sorted
                            order.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#collectionQuestionFive">

                            Does Map extend Collection?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="collectionQuestionFive"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#collectionQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            No. Map is a separate interface in the Collections
                            Framework and represents key-value associations.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SUMMARY -->

        <section class="mb-4">

            <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Summary</h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    The Java Collections Framework provides reusable data
                    structures for managing groups of objects. Important
                    interfaces include List, Set, Queue, Deque, and Map,
                    with implementations such as ArrayList, HashSet,
                    TreeSet, HashMap, TreeMap, and ArrayDeque.
                </p>

            </div>

